  #line-at(78pt, 234.36pt, "Ash content in biomass feedstock plays a decisive role in both thermochemical conversion ")
  #line-at(78pt, 255.12pt, "processes and pelletization performance. In pyrolysis, a higher ash percentage leads to a ")
  #line-at(78pt, 275.76pt, "lower yield of biochar, since ashes are non-combustible residues that reduce the amount of ")
  #line-at(78pt, 296.52pt, "fixed carbon and affect the reactivity of the resulting biochar in subsequent gasification ")
  #line-at(78pt, 317.16pt, "processes. Moreover, the mineral components of ash catalyze secondary cracking reactions ")
  #line-at(78pt, 337.92pt, "that degrade bio-oil, lowering its stability and calorific quality [39,40]. Conversely, in ")
  #line-at(78pt, 358.56pt, "pelletization, while excessive ash can reduce the calorific value and increase slagging ")
  #line-at(78pt, 379.32pt, "during combustion, a moderate ash content may exhibit synergistic effects on mechanical ")
  #line-at(78pt, 399.96pt, "properties, promoting greater compaction, reduced porosity, and enhanced durability. ")
  #line-at(78pt, 420.72pt, "Thus, although there is no direct correlation between ash content and mechanical ")
  #line-at(78pt, 441.36pt, "improvement, an optimal level of ash contributes to a denser and more homogeneous ")
  #line-at(78pt, 462.12pt, "microstructure, which favors pellet strength and handling stability [41]. ")
  #line-at(78pt, 496.8pt, "From the perspective of both pyrolysis efficiency and pellet quality, biomass species with ")
  #line-at(78pt, 517.44pt, "low ash content and high heating value (HHV) are the most suitable. Ideal candidates ")
